Part E: Find and Fix
1. What does grade(85) return, and why?
grade(85) returns 'C'. The if-else chain checks conditions from lowest to highest (mark >= 50 first); because 85 >= 50 is immediately true, the function returns 'C' without ever reaching the >= 70 or >= 80 branches.
2. What happens when you call isPalindrome('level')? Give the exact error type.
It throws a RangeError (specifically RangeError (index): Invalid value: Not in inclusive range 0..4: 5). On the first iteration when i = 0, word[word.length - i] evaluates to word[5], which is one index past the last valid index (4) of a 5-character string.
3. Fixed Functions and Changed Lines:
In grade(int mark), I reordered the condition thresholds from highest to lowest so higher marks are matched first:
• Changed if (mark >= 50) { return 'C'; } to if (mark >= 80) { return 'A'; }
• Changed } else if (mark >= 80) { return 'A'; } to } else if (mark >= 50) { return 'C'; }

In isPalindrome(String word), I fixed the off-by-one upper index, added lowercase normalization, and optimized the loop bound:
• Added String text = word.toLowerCase(); to normalize character case.
• Changed while (i < word.length) to while (i < text.length ~/ 2) so only half the string needs comparison.
• Changed if (word[i] != word[word.length - i]) return false; to if (text[i] != text[text.length - 1 - i]) { return false; } to fix the out-of-bounds index.
4. Case Sensitivity Choice for isPalindrome('Level'):
After the fix, isPalindrome('Level') returns true. In everyday English and standard user input, palindromes are evaluated by their letters rather than capitalization, so a capitalized word at the start of a sentence like 'Level' is still recognized as the same palindrome as 'level'. Converting the input with word.toLowerCase() ensures consistent, case-insensitive behavior.
