Part G: Debugging Log
Mistake 1: Off-by-one comma placement in formatRwf
Exact Error / Wrong Output: Wrong output: ,1,000 RWF and ,1,250,000 RWF (a leading comma appeared before numbers whose digit count was a multiple of 3, such as 100000 or 123456789).
Line / Location: In formatRwf(), inside the while loop: if (i % 3 == 0) { reversed += ','; } (before adding the i > 0 guard and adjusting index offset).
Cause: When iterating from right to left, checking i % 3 == 0 triggered at index i = 0 (the leftmost digit), appending a trailing comma to reversed which became a leading comma after reversing.
Fix: Added the condition i > 0 && (number.length - 1 - i + 1) % 3 == 0 (structured as i > 0 && i % 3 == 0 relative to digit position) so commas are only inserted between groups of three digits and never before the most significant digit.
Mistake 2: Index out-of-range error on whitespace-only input in initials
Exact Error / Wrong Output: Error message: RangeError (index): Invalid value: Valid value range is empty: 0
Line / Location: In initials(), on the line: result += '${words[i][0].toUpperCase()}.'; (before adding fullName.trim() and the isEmpty check).
Cause: Calling '   '.split(' ') returned a list of empty strings ['', '', '', ''], and attempting to access index [0] of an empty string threw a RangeError.
Fix: Added String name = fullName.trim(); followed by if (name.isEmpty) { return ''; } and used name.split(RegExp(r'\s+')) to cleanly collapse all extra spaces.
Mistake 3: Using a for loop instead of a while loop in Part D/F
Exact Error / Wrong Output: Wrong compliance / logic issue: Wrote for (int i = 1; i <= d + 3; i++) in main() and for (int i = 3; i < phone.length - 2; i++) in maskPhone().
Line / Location: In maskPhone() and main() (Part D2) and isValidPin() (Part F).
Cause: Habitually used standard for loops before double-checking Assignment Rule 2, which strictly mandates that every loop in Part D and Part F must be a while loop.
Fix: Refactored every for loop into an explicit while loop with manual counter initialization before the loop and counter increment (i++) at the end of the loop body.
Reflection: Which Question Took the Longest?
The MoMo USSD simulator, specifically formatRwf and isValidPin took the longest because implementing manual thousand-separators formatting when using only a while loop and basic string indexing required careful boundary checks for negative numbers and digit that counts divisible by three. Verifying all edge cases such as zero, negative values, repeating digits, and non-numeric characters also required several rounds of testing and adjustment.
